{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-89768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/432f8963425031983284db54310c8ae604bc993de66494dceb48e6a4e2bcdf73.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-7)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{b} & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f12c3bd899e22186d51d9f4fcdf2b7356e5d63dc82c04d86caf7f3f17998184"} {"question" "q-89768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=9\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/245037d572c2bc9c81d3cdfb7a485b167c15223c68e3dbb8e40eaa2cc7650da9.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-1)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f12c3bd899e22186d51d9f4fcdf2b7356e5d63dc82c04d86caf7f3f17998184"} {"question" "q-89774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-89774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f12c3bd899e22186d51d9f4fcdf2b7356e5d63dc82c04d86caf7f3f17998184"} {"question" "q-106623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/32375f839dd0f51a8624bebda732d7413130c940045db7e5a1a9fce3576e91b6.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/d44195c764b2943c3d5b5e0d91ee770011c951d7a825f0c5cc4d1f38cedbb709.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f12c3bd899e22186d51d9f4fcdf2b7356e5d63dc82c04d86caf7f3f17998184"} {"question" "q-106685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne 0"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/1474fb8efcada2aec61c1aa2b2ed16615e0ba968ec4e53a8fbfe05b1107c085f.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/46c9d9139a3cff2a76871c69deeac60492d2c9aef4bf1f7ac07a16868d31d6e0.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f12c3bd899e22186d51d9f4fcdf2b7356e5d63dc82c04d86caf7f3f17998184"} {"question" "q-106687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/state.json" "file_sha256" "70ce0ecfa029b8573bfc56994de302f8f19d65332a24f6ad26675122b66a5f8c"} {"question" "q-106687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne 0"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/